PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L07U3.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":7,"cefr":"B1+","unit":3,"theme":"Great Minds","essential_question":"What can we learn from the great thinkers of the past?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1","title":"Ibn al-Haytham, Who Studied Light: Non-Defining Relative Clauses","objectives":["Students will be able to add extra information about a person, thing, place or time with a non-defining relative clause.","Students will be able to punctuate non-defining clauses with commas and avoid that and extra pronouns.","I can write \"Ibn Sina, who was born near Bukhara, became a famous doctor.\"","I can choose who, which, whose, where or when."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من إضافة معلومات إضافية عن شخص أو شيء أو مكان أو زمن بجملة وصل غير محدِّدة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من ترقيم جمل الوصل غير المحدِّدة بالفواصل وتجنّب that والضمائر الزائدة.","أستطيع أن أكتب Ibn Sina, who was born near Bukhara, became a famous doctor.","أستطيع أن أختار بين who وwhich وwhose وwhere وwhen."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"scholar","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈskɑlɚ/","definition":"a person who studies a subject deeply and knows a lot about it","arabic":"عالِم / باحث","example":"Ibn Sina was a great scholar of medicine.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS1-W01"},{"word":"genius","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdʒinjəs/","definition":"a person who is extremely intelligent or talented","arabic":"عبقري","example":"Al-Khwarizmi was a mathematical genius.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS1-W02"},{"word":"discovery","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈskʌvɚi/","definition":"finding something that nobody knew before","arabic":"اكتشاف","example":"The discovery of penicillin saved millions of lives.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS1-W03"},{"word":"invention","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪnˈvɛnʃən/","definition":"a new machine or idea that someone creates","arabic":"اختراع","example":"The water clock was an amazing invention.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"A non-defining relative clause adds extra information about a noun that is already clear – usually a name, a place or a unique thing. The sentence is complete without it: Ibn al-Haytham, who was born in Basra, studied light. (Remove the clause → Ibn al-Haytham studied light. ✓)\nUse who for people, which for things and ideas, whose for possession (his/her/its), where for places and when for times. Put commas around the clause (or one comma before it at the end of a sentence).\nIn non-defining clauses you cannot use that, and you cannot leave out the relative pronoun. Don't add an extra pronoun: (not: Ibn Sina, who he wrote…) Arabic often repeats the pronoun (الكتاب الذي قرأتُه), but English doesn't: (not: the book, which I read it) → the book, which I read.\nwho = people • which = things/ideas • whose = possession • where = places • when = times\ncommas around the clause: X, who…, verb…\nno that • never leave out the pronoun\nno extra pronoun: (not: who he) / (not: which I read it)","examples":["Al-Khwarizmi, who worked in Baghdad, wrote a famous book on algebra.","The House of Wisdom, which was a huge library, attracted scholars from many lands.","Fatima al-Fihri, whose father was a rich merchant, built a university in Fez.","Córdoba, where Al-Zahrawi lived, was famous for its doctors.","In 1154, when Al-Idrisi finished his world map, few maps were so accurate.","Marie Curie, who won two Nobel Prizes, studied radioactivity.","The Canon of Medicine, which Ibn Sina wrote, was used in Europe for centuries.","My grandfather, whose hobby is astronomy, has a small telescope."],"common_errors":["✗ Ibn Sina who was a doctor wrote many books. → ✓ Ibn Sina, who was a doctor, wrote many books. (Extra information → commas.)","✗ Riyadh, that is the capital, is growing fast. → ✓ Riyadh, which is the capital, is growing fast. (No that in non-defining clauses.)","✗ Al-Biruni, who he lived in Ghazni, studied the stars. → ✓ Al-Biruni, who lived in Ghazni, studied the stars. (No extra pronoun after who.)","✗ The Book of Optics, which he wrote it in Cairo, is famous. → ✓ The Book of Optics, which he wrote in Cairo, is famous. (Don't repeat the object (it).)"],"arabic":"جملة الوصل غير المحدِّدة تضيف معلومة إضافية عن اسم معروف، والجملة كاملة بدونها: Ibn al-Haytham, who was born in Basra, studied light.\nwho للعاقل، وwhich لغير العاقل، وwhose للملكية، وwhere للمكان، وwhen للزمان، ونضع فواصل حول الجملة.\nلا نستخدم that فيها، ولا نحذف ضمير الوصل.\nفي العربية نكرر الضمير (الكتاب الذي قرأتُه)، أما في الإنجليزية فلا نكرره: the book, which I read."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Ibn al-Haytham, Who Studied Light: Non-Defining Relative Clauses”. Let’s learn non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause together.","on_screen":"GV-L07-U3-LS1 | Ibn al-Haytham, Who Studied Light: Non-Defining Relative Clauses","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “The Man Who Explained Light”: Ibn al-Haytham, who was born in Basra in 965, is often called the father of modern optics. Optics, which is the science of light and vision, fascinated him from a young age. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"Ibn al-Haytham, who was born in Basra in 965, is often called the father of modern optics. Optics, which is the science of light and vision, fascinated him from a young age.","visual":"Image GV-L07-U3-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"A non-defining relative clause adds extra information about a noun that is already clear – usually a name, a place or a unique thing. The sentence is complete without it: Ibn al-Haytham, who was born in Basra, studied light. (Remove the clause → Ibn al-Haytham studied light. ✓) Use who for people, which for things and ideas, whose for possession (his/her/its), where for places and when for times. Put commas around the clause (or one comma before it at the end of a sentence).","on_screen":"who = people • which = things/ideas • whose = possession • where = places • when = times • commas around the clause: X, who…, verb… • no that • never leave out the pronoun","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Al-Khwarizmi, who worked in Baghdad, wrote a famous book on algebra. The House of Wisdom, which was a huge library, attracted scholars from many lands. Fatima al-Fihri, whose father was a rich merchant, built a university in Fez.","on_screen":"Al-Khwarizmi, who worked in Baghdad, wrote a famous book on algebra. / The House of Wisdom, which was a huge library, attracted scholars from many lands. / Fatima al-Fihri, whose father was a rich merchant, built a university in Fez.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Ibn Sina who was a doctor wrote many books.” That’s not right. We say: “Ibn Sina, who was a doctor, wrote many books.” Extra information → commas.","on_screen":"✗ Ibn Sina who was a doctor wrote many books.   ✓ Ibn Sina, who was a doctor, wrote many books.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"Ibn Sina, who was born near Bukhara, became a doctor.\" Can we remove the clause and still have a full sentence? … Think! … Yes – it's extra information. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Ibn Sina, who was born near Bukhara, became a famous doctor.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Ibn Sina, who was born near Bukhara, became a famous doctor.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","جملة الوصل غير المحدِّدة تضيف معلومة إضافية عن اسم معروف، والجملة كاملة بدونها: Ibn al-Haytham, who was born in Basra, studied light.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Ibn Sina who was a doctor wrote many books. — الصحيح: Ibn Sina, who was a doctor, wrote many books.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Ibn Sina, who was born near Bukhara, became a famous doctor."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"The Man Who Explained Light","body":"Ibn al-Haytham, who was born in Basra in 965, is often called the father of modern optics. Optics, which is the science of light and vision, fascinated him from a young age.\nAt that time, many thinkers believed that our eyes sent out rays of light. Ibn al-Haytham, whose ideas were based on careful experiments, disagreed. He showed that we see objects because light travels from them into our eyes.\nLater, he moved to Cairo, where he spent most of his life. There, he wrote the Book of Optics, which had seven volumes. In it, he described the camera obscura, which is a dark room with a tiny hole in one wall. The light that enters through the hole makes an upside-down picture on the opposite wall.\nHis method was simple but powerful: ask a question, test it with an experiment, and then check the results. Today, this method, which we call the scientific method, is used in laboratories all over the world.\nHis book was translated into Latin around 1200, when European scholars were hungry for new knowledge. Many later scientists learned from his work.\nIbn al-Haytham once wrote that a true seeker of knowledge should question everything he reads. That advice is still useful for students today.","word_count":209,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Ibn Sina, ___ was born near Bukhara, became a famous doctor.","options":["whose","which","where","who"],"answer":"who","explanation":"Person → who.","explanation_ar":"للعاقل who."},{"q":"The Canon of Medicine, ___ Ibn Sina wrote, was used for centuries.","options":["whose","who","which","where"],"answer":"which","explanation":"Thing or idea → which (not that).","explanation_ar":"لغير العاقل which."},{"q":"Al-Khwarizmi, ___ name gave us the word \"algorithm,\" worked in Baghdad.","options":["that","which","whose","who"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"Possession (his/her/its) → whose.","explanation_ar":"للملكية whose."},{"q":"Fez, ___ Fatima al-Fihri founded a university, is in Morocco.","options":["where","who","when","which"],"answer":"where","explanation":"Place → where.","explanation_ar":"للمكان where."},{"q":"The year 1206, ___ Al-Jazari finished his book of machines, is important in engineering history.","options":["where","when","which","who"],"answer":"when","explanation":"Time → when.","explanation_ar":"للزمان when."},{"q":"","options":["Marie Curie, who she won two Nobel Prizes, was born in Warsaw.","Marie Curie, that won two Nobel Prizes, was born in Warsaw.","Marie Curie who won two Nobel Prizes was born in Warsaw.","Marie Curie, who won two Nobel Prizes, was born in Warsaw."],"answer":"Marie Curie, who won two Nobel Prizes, was born in Warsaw.","explanation":"Commas around the extra information; no extra pronoun.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل ولا ضمير زائد."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Write who, which, whose, where or when. Word bank: which, whose, where","items":[{"q":"Baghdad, ______ the House of Wisdom stood, was a great center of learning.","options":[],"answer":"where","explanation":"Place → where.","explanation_ar":"للمكان where."},{"q":"Ibn al-Haytham, ______ experiments changed science, lived in Cairo.","options":[],"answer":"whose","explanation":"Possession (his/her/its) → whose.","explanation_ar":"للملكية whose."},{"q":"Algebra, ______ many students find difficult, takes its name from an Arabic word.","options":[],"answer":"which","explanation":"Thing or idea → which (not that).","explanation_ar":"لغير العاقل which."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Join the sentences with a non-defining relative clause.","items":[{"q":"Al-Biruni measured the size of the Earth.  +  He lived over a thousand years ago. (who)","options":[],"answer":"Al-Biruni, who lived over a thousand years ago, measured the size of the Earth.","explanation":"Person → who.","explanation_ar":"للعاقل who."},{"q":"The elephant clock was designed by Al-Jazari.  +  It used water to measure time. (which)","options":[],"answer":"The elephant clock, which used water to measure time, was designed by Al-Jazari.","explanation":"Thing or idea → which (not that).","explanation_ar":"لغير العاقل which."},{"q":"Al-Zahrawi described about 200 surgical tools.  +  His book was used in Europe for centuries. (whose)","options":[],"answer":"Al-Zahrawi, whose book was used in Europe for centuries, described about 200 surgical tools.","explanation":"Possession (his/her/its) → whose.","explanation_ar":"للملكية whose."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Add extra information.","items":[{"q":"My school, which ___, is in ___.","options":[],"answer":"My school, which opened in 2007, is in Taif.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"My best friend, who ___, wants to be ___.","options":[],"answer":"My best friend, who loves chemistry, wants to be a pharmacist.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write about a city using where.","options":[],"answer":"Makkah, where millions of pilgrims go every year, is a holy city.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write about a person using whose.","options":[],"answer":"My aunt, whose job is in a hospital lab, analyzes blood samples.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write about a year using when.","options":[],"answer":"2017, when my little brother was born, was a happy year for my family.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Mini-biography.","items":[{"q":"Choose a scientist or scholar. Write 2 sentences with who and which.","options":[],"answer":"Al-Idrisi, who was a geographer, made a famous world map. His map, which he finished in 1154, showed Europe, Asia and Africa.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Add one sentence with where or when.","options":[],"answer":"He worked in Palermo, where the king of Sicily supported his work.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Add one sentence with whose.","options":[],"answer":"Al-Idrisi, whose maps were very detailed, is still remembered today.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Ball Toss Sentences","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["Students stand in a circle. The teacher says a cue word and throws the ball.","The catcher makes a sentence with the cue word and non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause.","If it is correct, the catcher chooses the next cue word and throws the ball to a new student.","If it is not correct, classmates help fix it, and the same student tries again."],"data":[{"label":", who… ,"},{"label":", which… ,"},{"label":", whose… ,"},{"label":", where… ,"},{"label":", when… ,"},{"label":", which surprised everyone"},{"label":"(born in 980)"},{"label":"—the father of algebra—"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Al-Biruni, ___ lived in the eleventh century, studied astronomy.","options":["whose","which","who","when"],"answer":"who","explanation":"Person → who.","explanation_ar":"للعاقل who."},{"q":"The Book of Optics, ___ had seven volumes, was translated into Latin.","options":["whose","which","where","who"],"answer":"which","explanation":"Thing or idea → which (not that).","explanation_ar":"لغير العاقل which."},{"q":"Ibn Rushd, ___ books influenced European thinkers, lived in Córdoba.","options":["whose","that","who","which"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"Possession (his/her/its) → whose.","explanation_ar":"للملكية whose."},{"q":"Cairo, ___ Ibn al-Haytham spent most of his life, has many old libraries.","options":["when","whose","which","where"],"answer":"where","explanation":"Place → where.","explanation_ar":"للمكان where."},{"q":"","options":["My uncle, who he is an engineer, fixed our solar panels.","My uncle, who is an engineer, fixed our solar panels.","My uncle, that is an engineer, fixed our solar panels.","My uncle who is an engineer, fixed our solar panels."],"answer":"My uncle, who is an engineer, fixed our solar panels.","explanation":"Commas around the extra information; no extra pronoun.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل ولا ضمير زائد."},{"q":"Ramadan, ___ Muslims fast from dawn to sunset, is a special month.","options":["where","who","which","when"],"answer":"when","explanation":"Time → when.","explanation_ar":"للزمان when."},{"q":"","options":["Mars, which is called the Red Planet, is smaller than Earth.","Mars which is called the Red Planet is smaller than Earth.","Mars, which it is called the Red Planet, is smaller than Earth.","Mars, that is called the Red Planet, is smaller than Earth."],"answer":"Mars, which is called the Red Planet, is smaller than Earth.","explanation":"Thing or idea → which (not that).","explanation_ar":"لغير العاقل which."},{"q":"","options":["The manuscript, my grandfather found, is three hundred years old.","The manuscript, which my grandfather found it, is three hundred years old.","The manuscript, which my grandfather found, is three hundred years old.","The manuscript, that my grandfather found, is three hundred years old."],"answer":"The manuscript, which my grandfather found, is three hundred years old.","explanation":"Commas around the extra information; no extra pronoun.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل ولا ضمير زائد."},{"q":"Al-Idrisi, ___ map was made for a king, traveled widely.","options":["which","who","who's","whose"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"Possession (his/her/its) → whose.","explanation_ar":"للملكية whose."},{"q":"Which sentence has a non-defining relative clause?","options":["Jeddah, which is on the Red Sea, is a busy port.","The sailors who work there are friendly.","The ship which we saw was huge.","The port that we visited was very busy."],"answer":"Jeddah, which is on the Red Sea, is a busy port.","explanation":"Commas around the extra information; no extra pronoun.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل ولا ضمير زائد."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"A medieval scholar's study in Cairo: Ibn al-Haytham (shown respectfully from behind, no face) at a wooden desk with glass lenses, a candle and a dark room with a small hole projecting an upside-down image of a palm tree onto the wall; open manuscripts with diagrams of the eye. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A medieval scholar's study in Cairo: Ibn al-Haytham (shown respectfully from behind, no face) at a wooden desk with glass lenses, a candle and a dark room with a small hole projecting an upside-down image of a palm tree onto the wall; open manuscripts with diagrams of the eye"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause, with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows scholar (a person who studies a subject deeply and knows a lot about it); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing scholar: a person who studies a subject deeply and knows a lot about it"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows genius (a person who is extremely intelligent or talented); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing genius: a person who is extremely intelligent or talented"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows discovery (finding something that nobody knew before); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing discovery: finding something that nobody knew before"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows invention (a new machine or idea that someone creates); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing invention: a new machine or idea that someone creates"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Ball Toss Sentences”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Ball Toss Sentences"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS1-W01","script":"scholar. scholar. Ibn Sina was a great scholar of medicine.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS1-W02","script":"genius. genius. Al-Khwarizmi was a mathematical genius.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS1-W03","script":"discovery. discovery. The discovery of penicillin saved millions of lives.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS1-W04","script":"invention. invention. The water clock was an amazing invention.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS1-T1","script":"Ibn al-Haytham, who was born in Basra in 965, is often called the father of modern optics. Optics, which is the science of light and vision, fascinated him from a young age. At that time, many thinkers believed that our eyes sent out rays of light. Ibn al-Haytham, whose ideas were based on careful experiments, disagreed. He showed that we see objects because light travels from them into our eyes. Later, he moved to Cairo, where he spent most of his life. There, he wrote the Book of Optics, which had seven volumes. In it, he described the camera obscura, which is a dark room with a tiny hole in one wall. The light that enters through the hole makes an upside-down picture on the opposite wall. His method was simple but powerful: ask a question, test it with an experiment, and then check the results. Today, this method, which we call the scientific method, is used in laboratories all over the world. His book was translated into Latin around 1200, when European scholars were hungry for new knowledge. Many later scientists learned from his work. Ibn al-Haytham once wrote that a true seeker of knowledge should question everything he reads. That advice is still useful for students today.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Ibn al-Haytham, Who Studied Light: Non-Defining Relative Clauses”. Let’s learn non-defining relative clauses with who, which, whose, where and when; commas around the clause; no that; the relative pronoun can't be left out; no extra pronoun inside the clause together. Listen to the start of “The Man Who Explained Light”: Ibn al-Haytham, who was born in Basra in 965, is often called the father of modern optics. Optics, which is the science of light and vision, fascinated him from a young age. Look at the green words. A non-defining relative clause adds extra information about a noun that is already clear – usually a name, a place or a unique thing. The sentence is complete without it: Ibn al-Haytham, who was born in Basra, studied light. (Remove the clause → Ibn al-Haytham studied light. ✓) Use who for people, which for things and ideas, whose for possession (his/her/its), where for places and when for times. Put commas around the clause (or one comma before it at the end of a sentence). Here are some examples. Al-Khwarizmi, who worked in Baghdad, wrote a famous book on algebra. The House of Wisdom, which was a huge library, attracted scholars from many lands. Fatima al-Fihri, whose father was a rich merchant, built a university in Fez. Be careful! Some learners say: “Ibn Sina who was a doctor wrote many books.” That’s not right. We say: “Ibn Sina, who was a doctor, wrote many books.” Extra information → commas. Quick check: \"Ibn Sina, who was born near Bukhara, became a doctor.\" Can we remove the clause and still have a full sentence? … Think! … Yes – it's extra information. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Ibn Sina, who was born near Bukhara, became a famous doctor.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2","title":"The Scientist Who… or Ibn Sina, Who…? Defining vs Non-Defining Clauses","objectives":["Students will be able to tell defining clauses (which one?) from non-defining clauses (extra information) and punctuate them correctly.","Students will be able to explain how commas change meaning and use which to comment on a whole clause.","I can explain the difference between \"my brother who lives in Jeddah\" and \"my brother, who lives in Jeddah,\".","I can write \"He passed the test, which surprised everyone.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من التمييز بين جمل الوصل المحدِّدة (أيّ واحد؟) وغير المحدِّدة (معلومة إضافية) وترقيمها بشكل صحيح.","سيتمكن الطلاب من شرح كيف تغيّر الفواصل المعنى واستخدام which للتعليق على جملة كاملة.","أستطيع أن أشرح الفرق بين my brother who lives in Jeddah وmy brother, who lives in Jeddah,","أستطيع أن أكتب He passed the test, which surprised everyone."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"experiment","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪkˈspɛrəmənt/","definition":"a scientific test to find out if an idea is true","arabic":"تجربة علمية","example":"We did an experiment with light and mirrors.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS2-W01"},{"word":"theory","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈθɪri/","definition":"an idea that tries to explain why something happens","arabic":"نظرية","example":"His theory explained how we see.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS2-W02"},{"word":"evidence","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɛvədəns/","definition":"facts that show something is true","arabic":"دليل / برهان","example":"Scientists need evidence to support their ideas.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS2-W03"},{"word":"observe","pos":"verb","ipa":"/əbˈzɝv/","definition":"to watch something carefully to learn about it","arabic":"يلاحظ / يرصد","example":"Al-Biruni observed the sky for many years.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"A defining clause tells us which one we mean. It is necessary, so there are no commas: The scientist who discovered penicillin was Alexander Fleming. A non-defining clause gives extra information about someone already clear, so we use commas: Ibn Sina, who was a doctor, wrote many books.\nIn defining clauses we can use that instead of who/which, and we can leave out the pronoun when it is the object: The book (that) I borrowed is about Ibn Sina. In non-defining clauses we can't use that and can't leave out the pronoun.\nCommas change meaning: My sister who studies medicine lives in Riyadh. (I have more than one sister.) ↔ My sister, who studies medicine, lives in Riyadh. (I have only one sister.) We can also use , which to comment on a whole clause: He got full marks, which surprised everyone.\ndefining: which one? • no commas • that OK • object pronoun can go\nnon-defining: extra • commas • no that • pronoun stays\ncommas = only one / already clear; no commas = one of several\n, which + comment on the whole idea","examples":["The boy who sits next to me wants to be an astronaut.","Saturn, which has beautiful rings, is the sixth planet from the Sun.","The book (that) I'm reading is about Al-Biruni.","The museum where we saw the manuscripts is in Riyadh.","Riyadh, which is the capital, has excellent museums.","We lost the match, which made the coach angry.","Students whose projects win will go to the national fair.","Ibn Battuta, whose journeys took almost thirty years, wrote a famous travel book."],"common_errors":["✗ The scientist, who discovered penicillin, was Fleming. → ✓ The scientist who discovered penicillin was Fleming. (Which scientist? → defining, no commas.)","✗ Saturn which has rings is beautiful. → ✓ Saturn, which has rings, is beautiful. (Only one Saturn → non-defining, commas.)","✗ He won the prize, that surprised us. → ✓ He won the prize, which surprised us. (Comment on the whole clause → , which.)","✗ Riyadh, I visited last year, is huge. → ✓ Riyadh, which I visited last year, is huge. (Non-defining → don't leave out the pronoun.)"],"arabic":"الجملة المحدِّدة تحدد أيّ واحد نقصد، وهي ضرورية فلا فواصل: The scientist who discovered penicillin was Fleming.\nالجملة غير المحدِّدة معلومة إضافية عن شيء معروف، فنضع فواصل: Ibn Sina, who was a doctor, wrote many books.\nفي المحدِّدة يمكن استخدام that وحذف ضمير المفعول، أما غير المحدِّدة فلا.\nالفواصل تغيّر المعنى: My sister, who studies medicine, … (لي أخت واحدة) / My sister who studies medicine … (لي أكثر من أخت)."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Scientist Who… or Ibn Sina, Who…? Defining vs Non-Defining Clauses”. Let’s learn defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns; sentence-level which (…, which surprised everyone) together.","on_screen":"GV-L07-U3-LS2 | The Scientist Who… or Ibn Sina, Who…? Defining vs Non-Defining Clauses","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: The lab that we use has new microscopes. • Mars, which has two moons, is red. The students who didn't finish can stay after class. Who is the girl who won the prize?","on_screen":"The lab that we use has new microscopes. • Mars, which has two moons, is red. | The students who didn't finish can stay after class. | Who is the girl who won the prize?","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"A defining clause tells us which one we mean. It is necessary, so there are no commas: The scientist who discovered penicillin was Alexander Fleming. A non-defining clause gives extra information about someone already clear, so we use commas: Ibn Sina, who was a doctor, wrote many books. In defining clauses we can use that instead of who/which, and we can leave out the pronoun when it is the object: The book (that) I borrowed is about Ibn Sina. In non-defining clauses we can't use that and can't leave out the pronoun.","on_screen":"defining: which one? • no commas • that OK • object pronoun can go • non-defining: extra • commas • no that • pronoun stays • commas = only one / already clear; no commas = one of several","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The boy who sits next to me wants to be an astronaut. Saturn, which has beautiful rings, is the sixth planet from the Sun. The book (that) I'm reading is about Al-Biruni.","on_screen":"The boy who sits next to me wants to be an astronaut. / Saturn, which has beautiful rings, is the sixth planet from the Sun. / The book (that) I'm reading is about Al-Biruni.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The scientist, who discovered penicillin, was Fleming.” That’s not right. We say: “The scientist who discovered penicillin was Fleming.” Which scientist? → defining, no commas.","on_screen":"✗ The scientist, who discovered penicillin, was Fleming.   ✓ The scientist who discovered penicillin was Fleming.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"My brother who lives in Jeddah is a doctor.\" How many brothers do I have? … Think! … More than one. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain the difference between \"my brother who lives in Jeddah\" and \"my brother, who lives in Jeddah,\".","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can explain the difference between \"my brother who lives in Jeddah\" and \"my brother, who lives in Jeddah,\".","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns; sentence-level which (…, which surprised everyone).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الجملة المحدِّدة تحدد أيّ واحد نقصد، وهي ضرورية فلا فواصل: The scientist who discovered penicillin was Fleming.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The scientist, who discovered penicillin, was Fleming. — الصحيح: The scientist who discovered penicillin was Fleming.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أشرح الفرق بين my brother who lives in Jeddah وmy brother, who lives in Jeddah,"]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"","options":["The scientist, who discovered penicillin, was Alexander Fleming.","The scientist who he discovered penicillin was Alexander Fleming.","The scientist which discovered penicillin was Alexander Fleming.","The scientist who discovered penicillin was Alexander Fleming."],"answer":"The scientist who discovered penicillin was Alexander Fleming.","explanation":"Defining (which one?) → no commas.","explanation_ar":"المحدِّدة بلا فواصل."},{"q":"","options":["My grandfather, who taught physics for forty years loves astronomy.","My grandfather who taught physics for forty years loves astronomy.","My grandfather, that taught physics for forty years, loves astronomy.","My grandfather, who taught physics for forty years, loves astronomy."],"answer":"My grandfather, who taught physics for forty years, loves astronomy.","explanation":"Non-defining (extra) → commas.","explanation_ar":"غير المحدِّدة بفواصل."},{"q":"\"My sister who studies medicine lives in Riyadh.\" What does this tell us?","options":["I have only one sister.","My sister lives with me.","I have more than one sister.","My sister doesn't study."],"answer":"I have more than one sister.","explanation":"Commas change the meaning.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل تغيّر المعنى."},{"q":"In which sentence is the relative pronoun optional?","options":["Riyadh, which is the capital, is very large.","Ibn Sina, who wrote many books, was a doctor.","The book that I borrowed is about Ibn Sina.","The student who won the prize is my cousin."],"answer":"The book that I borrowed is about Ibn Sina.","explanation":"Leave out the object pronoun only in defining clauses.","explanation_ar":"الحذف في المحدِّدة فقط."},{"q":"Which sentence cannot use \"that\"?","options":["Al-Ula, which has ancient tombs, attracts many tourists.","The tombs which we visited were amazing.","The guide who showed us around was friendly.","The photos which I took are on my laptop."],"answer":"Al-Ula, which has ancient tombs, attracts many tourists.","explanation":"that only in defining clauses.","explanation_ar":"that في المحدِّدة فقط."},{"q":"Ibn Sina wrote hundreds of books, ___ is amazing.","options":["that","which","what","who"],"answer":"which","explanation":", which comments on the whole clause.","explanation_ar":"which للتعليق على الجملة كلها."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Defining (D) or non-defining (N)? Write D or N. Word bank: N, D","items":[{"q":"The boy who sits next to me wants to be an astronaut. → ______","options":[],"answer":"D","explanation":"Defining (which one?) → no commas.","explanation_ar":"المحدِّدة بلا فواصل."},{"q":"Mars, which is called the Red Planet, has two moons. → ______","options":[],"answer":"N","explanation":"Non-defining (extra) → commas.","explanation_ar":"غير المحدِّدة بفواصل."},{"q":"The lab where we do experiments has new microscopes. → ______","options":[],"answer":"D","explanation":"Defining (which one?) → no commas.","explanation_ar":"المحدِّدة بلا فواصل."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Add commas where they are needed.","items":[{"q":"Ibn Battuta who traveled for almost thirty years wrote about his journeys.  (commas)","options":[],"answer":"Ibn Battuta, who traveled for almost thirty years, wrote about his journeys.","explanation":"Non-defining (extra) → commas.","explanation_ar":"غير المحدِّدة بفواصل."},{"q":"Our science teacher whose lessons are always fun took us to the planetarium.  (commas)","options":[],"answer":"Our science teacher, whose lessons are always fun, took us to the planetarium.","explanation":"Non-defining (extra) → commas.","explanation_ar":"غير المحدِّدة بفواصل."},{"q":"My best friend got full marks in science, and this made his parents proud. (which)","options":[],"answer":"My best friend got full marks in science, which made his parents proud.","explanation":", which comments on the whole clause.","explanation_ar":"which للتعليق على الجملة كلها."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Defining or non-defining?","items":[{"q":"Write a defining clause about a person in your class.","options":[],"answer":"The student who sits by the window is the class captain.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns; sentence-level which (…, which surprised everyone) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a non-defining clause about your city.","options":[],"answer":"Taif, which is famous for roses, is cool in summer.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns; sentence-level which (…, which surprised everyone) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence where you leave out \"that\".","options":[],"answer":"The app I use for revision is free.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns; sentence-level which (…, which surprised everyone) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \", which\" that comments on a whole idea.","options":[],"answer":"It rained during the match, which made the pitch slippery.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns; sentence-level which (…, which surprised everyone) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write two sentences that show how commas change the meaning.","options":[],"answer":"My cousin who lives in Dubai is an architect. (I have other cousins.) My cousin, who lives in Dubai, is an architect. (I have only one cousin.)","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns; sentence-level which (…, which surprised everyone) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Science fair descriptions.","items":[{"q":"Describe 2 projects at a science fair with defining clauses.","options":[],"answer":"The project that won first prize was a solar oven. The boy who made the robot arm is in Grade 9.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns; sentence-level which (…, which surprised everyone) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe 2 famous scientists with non-defining clauses.","options":[],"answer":"Ibn al-Haytham, who studied light, lived in Cairo. Marie Curie, whose research was about radioactivity, won two Nobel Prizes.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns; sentence-level which (…, which surprised everyone) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \", which\" about a result.","options":[],"answer":"Our volcano didn't erupt, which made everyone laugh.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns; sentence-level which (…, which surprised everyone) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Dice Sentences","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["Turn over a cue card and roll the die.","Make a sentence with the cue in the form shown by the die.","The player on the left checks; a correct sentence wins the card.","The player with the most cards after 7 minutes wins."],"data":[{"cue":"Ibn Sina / wrote The Canon of Medicine / born near Bukhara"},{"cue":"Fez / is in Morocco / Fatima al-Fihri built a university there"},{"cue":"Al-Khwarizmi / his name gave us \"algorithm\" / worked in Baghdad"},{"cue":"1154 / Al-Idrisi finished his world map then"},{"cue":"optics / the science of light"},{"cue":"Marie Curie / won two Nobel Prizes / born in Warsaw"},{"cue":"my uncle / is a scientist / lives in Dhahran"},{"cue":"the telescope / Galileo improved it in 1609"},{"cue":"the House of Wisdom / scholars translated books there"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"","options":["The student, who answered first, got a prize.","The student who answered first got a prize.","The student who he answered first got a prize.","The student which answered first got a prize."],"answer":"The student who answered first got a prize.","explanation":"Defining (which one?) → no commas.","explanation_ar":"المحدِّدة بلا فواصل."},{"q":"","options":["Al-Ahsa, which has millions of palm trees is a huge oasis.","Al-Ahsa which has millions of palm trees is a huge oasis.","Al-Ahsa, which has millions of palm trees, is a huge oasis.","Al-Ahsa, that has millions of palm trees, is a huge oasis."],"answer":"Al-Ahsa, which has millions of palm trees, is a huge oasis.","explanation":"Non-defining (extra) → commas.","explanation_ar":"غير المحدِّدة بفواصل."},{"q":"\"The players, who were tired, went home.\" What does it mean?","options":["All the players were tired, and they all went home.","Only some players were tired.","None of the players were tired.","The players stayed."],"answer":"All the players were tired, and they all went home.","explanation":"Commas change the meaning.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل تغيّر المعنى."},{"q":"Which pronoun can we leave out? \"The microscope ___ we used was broken.\"","options":["whose","(We can't leave it out.)","that","who"],"answer":"that","explanation":"Leave out the object pronoun only in defining clauses.","explanation_ar":"الحذف في المحدِّدة فقط."},{"q":"Our team won the final, ___ made the whole school proud.","options":["that","who","which","what"],"answer":"which","explanation":", which comments on the whole clause.","explanation_ar":"which للتعليق على الجملة كلها."},{"q":"","options":["Jupiter, is the largest planet, has many moons.","Jupiter which is the largest planet has many moons.","Jupiter, that is the largest planet, has many moons.","Jupiter, which is the largest planet, has many moons."],"answer":"Jupiter, which is the largest planet, has many moons.","explanation":"that only in defining clauses.","explanation_ar":"that في المحدِّدة فقط."},{"q":"","options":["The scholar whose book I'm reading it lived in Baghdad.","The scholar whose book I'm reading lived in Baghdad.","The scholar who's book I'm reading lived in Baghdad.","The scholar, whose book I'm reading, lived in Baghdad."],"answer":"The scholar whose book I'm reading lived in Baghdad.","explanation":"Defining (which one?) → no commas.","explanation_ar":"المحدِّدة بلا فواصل."},{"q":"Which sentence needs commas?","options":["Our principal who started at this school in 2010 is very kind.","The teacher who helped me is kind.","The pen that I lost was blue.","The city where I was born is small."],"answer":"Our principal who started at this school in 2010 is very kind.","explanation":"Non-defining (extra) → commas.","explanation_ar":"غير المحدِّدة بفواصل."},{"q":"\"My uncle, who works in Dhahran, is a geologist.\" How many uncles does the speaker probably have?","options":["two or more","We can't know anything.","none","one"],"answer":"one","explanation":"Commas change the meaning.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل تغيّر المعنى."},{"q":"","options":["I forgot my calculator, what was a big problem in the math test.","I forgot my calculator, which was a big problem in the math test.","I forgot my calculator, that was a big problem in the math test.","I forgot my calculator which it was a big problem in the math test."],"answer":"I forgot my calculator, which was a big problem in the math test.","explanation":", which comments on the whole clause.","explanation_ar":"which للتعليق على الجملة كلها."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"A science fair: Omar pointing to \"the student who built the volcano\" among several students, while a poster says \"Ibn Sina, who wrote The Canon of Medicine, …\"; two brothers shown as a family tree to explain commas and meaning. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A science fair: Omar pointing to \"the student who built the volcano\" among several students, while a poster says \"Ibn Sina, who wrote The Canon of Medicine, …\"; two brothers shown as a family tree to explain commas and meaning"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns; sentence-level which (…, which surprised everyone): a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Great Minds”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows experiment (a scientific test to find out if an idea is true); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing experiment: a scientific test to find out if an idea is true"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows theory (an idea that tries to explain why something happens); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing theory: an idea that tries to explain why something happens"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows evidence (facts that show something is true); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing evidence: facts that show something is true"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows observe (to watch something carefully to learn about it); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing observe: to watch something carefully to learn about it"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Dice Sentences”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Dice Sentences"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS2-W01","script":"experiment. experiment. We did an experiment with light and mirrors.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS2-W02","script":"theory. theory. His theory explained how we see.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS2-W03","script":"evidence. evidence. Scientists need evidence to support their ideas.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS2-W04","script":"observe. observe. Al-Biruni observed the sky for many years.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Scientist Who… or Ibn Sina, Who…? Defining vs Non-Defining Clauses”. Let’s learn defining vs non-defining relative clauses: meaning, commas, that, leaving out object pronouns; sentence-level which (…, which surprised everyone) together. Remember these patterns: The lab that we use has new microscopes. • Mars, which has two moons, is red. The students who didn't finish can stay after class. Who is the girl who won the prize? A defining clause tells us which one we mean. It is necessary, so there are no commas: The scientist who discovered penicillin was Alexander Fleming. A non-defining clause gives extra information about someone already clear, so we use commas: Ibn Sina, who was a doctor, wrote many books. In defining clauses we can use that instead of who/which, and we can leave out the pronoun when it is the object: The book (that) I borrowed is about Ibn Sina. In non-defining clauses we can't use that and can't leave out the pronoun. Here are some examples. The boy who sits next to me wants to be an astronaut. Saturn, which has beautiful rings, is the sixth planet from the Sun. The book (that) I'm reading is about Al-Biruni. Be careful! Some learners say: “The scientist, who discovered penicillin, was Fleming.” That’s not right. We say: “The scientist who discovered penicillin was Fleming.” Which scientist? → defining, no commas. Quick check: \"My brother who lives in Jeddah is a doctor.\" How many brothers do I have? … Think! … More than one. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain the difference between \"my brother who lives in Jeddah\" and \"my brother, who lives in Jeddah,\".","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3","title":"Scholar, Scientist, Scientific: Academic Word Families and Greek/Latin Roots","objectives":["Students will be able to use academic words about science and scholarship accurately.","Students will be able to use roots and affixes (astro-, geo-, bio-, tele-, -scope, -ology) to work out the meaning of new words.","I can use words like evidence, theory and contribution in a sentence.","I can guess that \"astronomy\" is about stars because \"astro\" means star."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام الكلمات الأكاديمية عن العلم والعلماء بدقة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام الجذور والزوائد (astro- وgeo- وbio- وtele- و-scope و-ology) لاستنتاج معنى الكلمات الجديدة.","أستطيع أن أستخدم كلمات مثل evidence وtheory وcontribution في جملة.","أستطيع أن أستنتج أن astronomy عن النجوم لأن astro تعني نجم."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"analyze","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈænəˌlaɪz/","definition":"to study something in detail to understand it","arabic":"يحلّل","example":"The students analyzed the results of the test.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-W01"},{"word":"contribution","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌkɑntrəˈbjuʃən/","definition":"something you give or do that helps a project or a field","arabic":"إسهام","example":"Muslim scholars made a huge contribution to science.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-W02"},{"word":"astronomy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈstrɑnəmi/","definition":"the scientific study of stars, planets and space","arabic":"علم الفلك","example":"Astronomy helped travelers find their way at night.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-W03"},{"word":"philosopher","pos":"noun","ipa":"/fəˈlɑsəfɚ/","definition":"a person who thinks deeply about life, truth and knowledge","arabic":"فيلسوف","example":"Ibn Rushd was a famous philosopher from Córdoba.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-W04"},{"word":"manuscript","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmænjəˌskrɪpt/","definition":"a very old book or document written by hand","arabic":"مخطوطة","example":"The library keeps rare Arabic manuscripts.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-W05"},{"word":"translate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/trænzˈleɪt/","definition":"to change words from one language into another","arabic":"يترجم","example":"His books were translated into Latin.","image_id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-W06"}],"explanation":{"text":"Academic words about science and learning: scholar, genius, discovery, invention, experiment, theory, evidence, observe, analyze, contribution, astronomy, philosopher, manuscript, translate.\nMany science words come from Greek and Latin roots: astro = star (astronomy), geo = earth (geography, geology), bio = life (biology, biography), micro = small (microscope), tele = far (telescope, telephone), scope = look at (microscope), graph = write (biography), -ology = the study of (biology, geology). Knowing the root helps you guess the meaning.\nWord families: discover → discovery → discoverer; invent → invention → inventor; analyze (v) → analysis (n) → analytical (adj); science → scientist → scientific → scientifically; contribute → contribution → contributor; evidence (n) → evident (adj). Note the American spelling analyze.\nWord families: discover: discovery (n), discoverer (n) | invent: invention (n), inventor (n), inventive (adj) | analyze: analysis (n), analytical (adj) | science: scientist (n), scientific (adj), scientifically (adv) | theory: theoretical (adj), theorist (n) | contribute: contribution (n), contributor (n)","examples":["Al-Khwarizmi's contribution to mathematics changed the world.","The scientific method starts with a question.","Ibn Sina was a genius who wrote about medicine, music and philosophy.","Without evidence, a theory is just an idea.","Our class analyzed the results of the experiment.","Geology is the study of rocks and the Earth.","A microscope helps us see very small things.","The library has a manuscript that is eight hundred years old."],"common_errors":["✗ He made a big discover. → ✓ He made a big discovery. (After a big → noun.)","✗ She is a famous invention. → ✓ She is a famous inventor. (The person → inventor.)","✗ We did a scientist experiment. → ✓ We did a scientific experiment. (Before a noun → adjective.)","✗ Biology is the study of stars. → ✓ Astronomy is the study of stars. (astro = star; bio = life.)"],"arabic":"كلمات أكاديمية: scholar (عالِم) وtheory (نظرية) وevidence (دليل) وcontribution (إسهام) وmanuscript (مخطوطة).\nجذور يونانية ولاتينية: astro (نجم)، geo (أرض)، bio (حياة)، micro (صغير)، tele (بعيد)، scope (ينظر)، graph (يكتب)، ology (علم).\nعائلات الكلمات: discover ← discovery، invent ← invention ← inventor، science ← scientist ← scientific.\nانتبه للتهجئة الأمريكية: analyze."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Scholar, Scientist, Scientific: Academic Word Families and Greek/Latin Roots”. Let’s learn academic vocabulary of science and learning; word families (discover → discovery; analyze → analysis; contribute → contribution); Greek/Latin roots (astro, geo, bio, micro, tele, scope, graph, -ology) together.","on_screen":"GV-L07-U3-LS3 | Scholar, Scientist, Scientific: Academic Word Families and Greek/Latin Roots","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. analyze: to study something in detail to understand it. contribution: something you give or do that helps a project or a field. astronomy: the scientific study of stars, planets and space.","on_screen":"analyze | contribution | astronomy","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Academic words about science and learning: scholar, genius, discovery, invention, experiment, theory, evidence, observe, analyze, contribution, astronomy, philosopher, manuscript, translate. Many science words come from Greek and Latin roots: astro = star (astronomy), geo = earth (geography, geology), bio = life (biology, biography), micro = small (microscope), tele = far (telescope, telephone), scope = look at (microscope), graph = write (biography), -ology = the study of (biology, geology). Knowing the root helps you guess the meaning.","on_screen":"academic vocabulary of science and learning; word families (discover → discovery; analyze → analysis; contribute → contribution); Greek/Latin roots (astro, geo, bio, micro, tele, scope, graph, -ology)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Al-Khwarizmi's contribution to mathematics changed the world. The scientific method starts with a question. Ibn Sina was a genius who wrote about medicine, music and philosophy.","on_screen":"Al-Khwarizmi's contribution to mathematics changed the world. / The scientific method starts with a question. / Ibn Sina was a genius who wrote about medicine, music and philosophy.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “He made a big discover.” That’s not right. We say: “He made a big discovery.” After a big → noun.","on_screen":"✗ He made a big discover.   ✓ He made a big discovery.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: What does \"tele\" mean in \"telescope\"? … Think! … far. Great job! Now you can say: I can use words like evidence, theory and contribution in a sentence.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can use words like evidence, theory and contribution in a sentence.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: academic vocabulary of science and learning; word families (discover → discovery; analyze → analysis; contribute → contribution); Greek/Latin roots (astro, geo, bio, micro, tele, scope, graph, -ology).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","كلمات أكاديمية: scholar (عالِم) وtheory (نظرية) وevidence (دليل) وcontribution (إسهام) وmanuscript (مخطوطة).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: He made a big discover. — الصحيح: He made a big discovery.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أستخدم كلمات مثل evidence وtheory وcontribution في جملة."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"Ibn al-Haytham did many ___ to test his ideas about light.","options":["manuscripts","philosophers","experiments","theories"],"answer":"experiments","explanation":"Choose the academic word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"A person who studies the stars and planets works in ___.","options":["astronomy","geography","geology","biology"],"answer":"astronomy","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root to find the meaning.","explanation_ar":"الجذر يدل على المعنى."},{"q":"Al-Khwarizmi's ___ to mathematics was enormous.","options":["contribute","contribution","contributor","contributive"],"answer":"contribution","explanation":"Choose the right word-family form.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الصحيحة."},{"q":"Scientists need ___ before they can say a theory is true.","options":["evidently","evidenced","evident","evidence"],"answer":"evidence","explanation":"Choose the right word-family form.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الصحيحة."},{"q":"The root \"tele\" in \"telescope\" means ___.","options":["small","life","far","earth"],"answer":"far","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root to find the meaning.","explanation_ar":"الجذر يدل على المعنى."},{"q":"Ibn Rushd was a ___ who wrote about logic and the meaning of life.","options":["philosopher","microscope","manuscript","laboratory"],"answer":"philosopher","explanation":"The word for the person (-er, -or, -ist, -ian).","explanation_ar":"كلمة الشخص."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the word to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"biology","options":["a person who studies a subject deeply","a very old book written by hand","a tool for looking at very small things","the study of rocks and the Earth","the written story of a person's life","the study of living things"],"answer":"the study of living things","explanation":"biology → the study of living things. Choose the academic word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"geology","options":["a person who studies a subject deeply","a very old book written by hand","a tool for looking at very small things","the study of rocks and the Earth","the written story of a person's life","the study of living things"],"answer":"the study of rocks and the Earth","explanation":"geology → the study of rocks and the Earth. Choose the academic word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"microscope","options":["a person who studies a subject deeply","a very old book written by hand","a tool for looking at very small things","the study of rocks and the Earth","the written story of a person's life","the study of living things"],"answer":"a tool for looking at very small things","explanation":"microscope → a tool for looking at very small things. Choose the academic word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"biography","options":["a person who studies a subject deeply","a very old book written by hand","a tool for looking at very small things","the study of rocks and the Earth","the written story of a person's life","the study of living things"],"answer":"the written story of a person's life","explanation":"biography → the written story of a person's life. Choose the academic word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"manuscript","options":["a person who studies a subject deeply","a very old book written by hand","a tool for looking at very small things","the study of rocks and the Earth","the written story of a person's life","the study of living things"],"answer":"a very old book written by hand","explanation":"manuscript → a very old book written by hand. Choose the academic word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"scholar","options":["a person who studies a subject deeply","a very old book written by hand","a tool for looking at very small things","the study of rocks and the Earth","the written story of a person's life","the study of living things"],"answer":"a person who studies a subject deeply","explanation":"scholar → a person who studies a subject deeply. Choose the academic word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Use academic words.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with \"evidence\".","options":[],"answer":"The police found evidence that the window was broken from outside.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic vocabulary of science and learning; word families (discover → discovery; analyze → analysis; contribute → contribution); Greek/Latin roots (astro, geo, bio, micro, tele, scope, graph, -ology) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"analyze\".","options":[],"answer":"We analyzed the temperature data from our weather station.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic vocabulary of science and learning; word families (discover → discovery; analyze → analysis; contribute → contribution); Greek/Latin roots (astro, geo, bio, micro, tele, scope, graph, -ology) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Name a discovery or an invention that changed the world.","options":[],"answer":"The invention of the printing press helped people share books.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic vocabulary of science and learning; word families (discover → discovery; analyze → analysis; contribute → contribution); Greek/Latin roots (astro, geo, bio, micro, tele, scope, graph, -ology) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a word that starts with \"geo\", \"bio\" or \"micro\" and explain it.","options":[],"answer":"Microbiology is the study of very small living things like bacteria.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic vocabulary of science and learning; word families (discover → discovery; analyze → analysis; contribute → contribution); Greek/Latin roots (astro, geo, bio, micro, tele, scope, graph, -ology) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe a contribution someone in your family has made.","options":[],"answer":"My father made a big contribution to our neighborhood by planting trees.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic vocabulary of science and learning; word families (discover → discovery; analyze → analysis; contribute → contribution); Greek/Latin roots (astro, geo, bio, micro, tele, scope, graph, -ology) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Great minds poster.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences about a scholar using 3 words from the unit.","options":[],"answer":"Al-Biruni was a scholar and a genius. He observed the sky for many years. His contribution to astronomy was huge.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic vocabulary of science and learning; word families (discover → discovery; analyze → analysis; contribute → contribution); Greek/Latin roots (astro, geo, bio, micro, tele, scope, graph, -ology) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences with \"scientist\" and \"scientific\".","options":[],"answer":"A scientist asks questions. She uses a scientific method to find the answers.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic vocabulary of science and learning; word families (discover → discovery; analyze → analysis; contribute → contribution); Greek/Latin roots (astro, geo, bio, micro, tele, scope, graph, -ology) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Make up a new \"-ology\" word for something you love and define it.","options":[],"answer":"Footballology – the study of football tactics.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic vocabulary of science and learning; word families (discover → discovery; analyze → analysis; contribute → contribution); Greek/Latin roots (astro, geo, bio, micro, tele, scope, graph, -ology) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Vocabulary Bingo","mechanic":"matching","rules":["Students write nine words from the list in their grid.","The teacher (or a student caller) reads a definition or Arabic meaning.","Students cross out the matching word.","Three in a row = \"Bingo!\" The winner must read the words and say what each means."],"data":[{"word":"scholar","match":"a person who studies a subject deeply and knows a lot about it","arabic":"عالِم / باحث"},{"word":"genius","match":"a person who is extremely intelligent or talented","arabic":"عبقري"},{"word":"discovery","match":"finding something that nobody knew before","arabic":"اكتشاف"},{"word":"invention","match":"a new machine or idea that someone creates","arabic":"اختراع"},{"word":"experiment","match":"a scientific test to find out if an idea is true","arabic":"تجربة علمية"},{"word":"theory","match":"an idea that tries to explain why something happens","arabic":"نظرية"},{"word":"evidence","match":"facts that show something is true","arabic":"دليل / برهان"},{"word":"observe","match":"to watch something carefully to learn about it","arabic":"يلاحظ / يرصد"},{"word":"analyze","match":"to study something in detail to understand it","arabic":"يحلّل"},{"word":"contribution","match":"something you give or do that helps a project or a field","arabic":"إسهام"},{"word":"astronomy","match":"the scientific study of stars, planets and space","arabic":"علم الفلك"},{"word":"philosopher","match":"a person who thinks deeply about life, truth and knowledge","arabic":"فيلسوف"},{"word":"manuscript","match":"a very old book or document written by hand","arabic":"مخطوطة"},{"word":"translate","match":"to change words from one language into another","arabic":"يترجم"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The ___ of the X-ray helped doctors see inside the body.","options":["discover","discoverer","discovery","discovered"],"answer":"discovery","explanation":"Choose the right word-family form.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الصحيحة."},{"q":"Thomas Edison was a famous ___.","options":["inventive","invent","invention","inventor"],"answer":"inventor","explanation":"The word for the person (-er, -or, -ist, -ian).","explanation_ar":"كلمة الشخص."},{"q":"We wrote a detailed ___ of our survey results.","options":["analysis","analyze","analyzed","analytical"],"answer":"analysis","explanation":"Choose the right word-family form.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الصحيحة."},{"q":"\"Bio\" in \"biology\" means ___.","options":["star","life","earth","write"],"answer":"life","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root to find the meaning.","explanation_ar":"الجذر يدل على المعنى."},{"q":"A ___ helps you see stars and planets far away.","options":["microscope","periscope","stethoscope","telescope"],"answer":"telescope","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root to find the meaning.","explanation_ar":"الجذر يدل على المعنى."},{"q":"The library protects old ___ that were written by hand.","options":["manuscripts","inventions","experiments","theories"],"answer":"manuscripts","explanation":"Choose the academic word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"Many Arabic books were ___ into Latin in the twelfth century.","options":["discovered","observed","translated","analyzed"],"answer":"translated","explanation":"Choose the academic word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"Her ___ explained why the experiment failed.","options":["theory","theorist","theorize","theoretical"],"answer":"theory","explanation":"Choose the right word-family form.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الصحيحة."},{"q":"A person who writes biographies or studies the past might be a ___.","options":["historic","historian","history","historical"],"answer":"historian","explanation":"The word for the person (-er, -or, -ist, -ian).","explanation_ar":"كلمة الشخص."},{"q":"Which word means \"the study of the Earth's rocks\"?","options":["astronomy","geology","biology","geography"],"answer":"geology","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root to find the meaning.","explanation_ar":"الجذر يدل على المعنى."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"An illustrated \"Root Tree\" poster in a library: roots labelled astro (star), geo (earth), bio (life), micro (small), tele (far), scope (look), graph (write) and -ology (study of), with branches showing astronomy, geology, biology, microscope, telescope and biography; an old astrolabe on a shelf. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"An illustrated \"Root Tree\" poster in a library: roots labelled astro (star), geo (earth), bio (life), micro (small), tele (far), scope (look), graph (write) and -ology (study of), with branches showing astronomy, geology, biology, microscope, telescope and biography; an old astrolabe on a shelf"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 6 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 6 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows analyze (to study something in detail to understand it); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing analyze: to study something in detail to understand it"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows contribution (something you give or do that helps a project or a field); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing contribution: something you give or do that helps a project or a field"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows astronomy (the scientific study of stars, planets and space); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing astronomy: the scientific study of stars, planets and space"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows philosopher (a person who thinks deeply about life, truth and knowledge); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing philosopher: a person who thinks deeply about life, truth and knowledge"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows manuscript (a very old book or document written by hand); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing manuscript: a very old book or document written by hand"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows translate (to change words from one language into another); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing translate: to change words from one language into another"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Vocabulary Bingo”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Vocabulary Bingo"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-W01","script":"analyze. analyze. The students analyzed the results of the test.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-W02","script":"contribution. contribution. Muslim scholars made a huge contribution to science.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-W03","script":"astronomy. astronomy. Astronomy helped travelers find their way at night.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-W04","script":"philosopher. philosopher. Ibn Rushd was a famous philosopher from Córdoba.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-W05","script":"manuscript. manuscript. The library keeps rare Arabic manuscripts.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-W06","script":"translate. translate. His books were translated into Latin.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Scholar, Scientist, Scientific: Academic Word Families and Greek/Latin Roots”. Let’s learn academic vocabulary of science and learning; word families (discover → discovery; analyze → analysis; contribute → contribution); Greek/Latin roots (astro, geo, bio, micro, tele, scope, graph, -ology) together. Meet some new words. analyze: to study something in detail to understand it. contribution: something you give or do that helps a project or a field. astronomy: the scientific study of stars, planets and space. Academic words about science and learning: scholar, genius, discovery, invention, experiment, theory, evidence, observe, analyze, contribution, astronomy, philosopher, manuscript, translate. Many science words come from Greek and Latin roots: astro = star (astronomy), geo = earth (geography, geology), bio = life (biology, biography), micro = small (microscope), tele = far (telescope, telephone), scope = look at (microscope), graph = write (biography), -ology = the study of (biology, geology). Knowing the root helps you guess the meaning. Here are some examples. Al-Khwarizmi's contribution to mathematics changed the world. The scientific method starts with a question. Ibn Sina was a genius who wrote about medicine, music and philosophy. Be careful! Some learners say: “He made a big discover.” That’s not right. We say: “He made a big discovery.” After a big → noun. Quick check: What does \"tele\" mean in \"telescope\"? … Think! … far. Great job! Now you can say: I can use words like evidence, theory and contribution in a sentence.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS4","title":"Al-Khwarizmi—the Father of Algebra—Lived in Baghdad: Commas, Parentheses and Dashes","objectives":["Students will be able to use commas, parentheses and dashes to set off extra information.","Students will be able to use the marks in pairs and leave essential information without commas.","I can write \"Al-Khwarizmi, a Persian mathematician, worked in Baghdad.\"","I can write \"Ibn Sina (980–1037) was a doctor\" and \"Her discovery—a new element—changed medicine.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام الفواصل والأقواس والشرطات لفصل المعلومات الإضافية.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام هذه العلامات في أزواج وترك المعلومات الضرورية دون فواصل.","أستطيع أن أكتب Al-Khwarizmi, a Persian mathematician, worked in Baghdad.","أستطيع أن أكتب Ibn Sina (980–1037) was a doctor وHer discovery—a new element—changed medicine."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"scholar","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈskɑlɚ/","definition":"a person who studies a subject deeply and knows a lot about it","arabic":"عالِم / باحث","example":"Ibn Sina was a great scholar of medicine.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"genius","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdʒinjəs/","definition":"a person who is extremely intelligent or talented","arabic":"عبقري","example":"Al-Khwarizmi was a mathematical genius.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"discovery","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈskʌvɚi/","definition":"finding something that nobody knew before","arabic":"اكتشاف","example":"The discovery of penicillin saved millions of lives.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"invention","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪnˈvɛnʃən/","definition":"a new machine or idea that someone creates","arabic":"اختراع","example":"The water clock was an amazing invention.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"experiment","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪkˈspɛrəmənt/","definition":"a scientific test to find out if an idea is true","arabic":"تجربة علمية","example":"We did an experiment with light and mirrors.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"theory","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈθɪri/","definition":"an idea that tries to explain why something happens","arabic":"نظرية","example":"His theory explained how we see.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Extra (nonrestrictive) information can be set off in three ways. Commas are the most common: Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad. (A noun phrase like this, which renames a noun, is called an appositive.)\nParentheses ( ) add information that is less important – dates, explanations or examples: Ibn Sina (980–1037) wrote about medicine. Dashes (—) make the extra information stronger or more dramatic: Her discovery—a new element—changed medicine. In American English, the long dash (—) usually has no spaces around it.\nUse the marks in pairs when the extra information is in the middle of the sentence – don't forget the second comma or dash. Don't put commas around essential (restrictive) information: The student who won the fair is in my class. (no commas)\ncommas: X, extra information, verb…\nparentheses: less important extra details, e.g., (980–1037)\ndashes: strong emphasis—like this—in the middle or at the end\nalways in pairs in the middle • no commas around essential information","examples":["Ibn Rushd, a philosopher from Córdoba, wrote about Aristotle.","Al-Biruni (973–1048) calculated the size of the Earth.","The answer was simple—it was water!","Our teacher—who loves astronomy—brought a telescope to class.","Fez, which is in Morocco, has a very old university.","The House of Wisdom (Bayt al-Hikma) was in Baghdad.","The student who built the robot is in Grade 8.","Ibn Battuta's travels—almost thirty years of them—took him to China."],"common_errors":["✗ Al-Khwarizmi a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad. → ✓ Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad. (Commas come in pairs.)","✗ Ibn Sina (980–1037 was a doctor. → ✓ Ibn Sina (980–1037) was a doctor. (Close the parentheses.)","✗ My cousin—a talented engineer, built a robot. → ✓ My cousin—a talented engineer—built a robot. (Match the marks: dash … dash.)","✗ The student, who won the fair, is in my class. → ✓ The student who won the fair is in my class. (Essential information → no commas.)"],"arabic":"المعلومة الإضافية نفصلها بالفواصل غالبًا: Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad.\nالأقواس لمعلومات أقل أهمية مثل التواريخ: Ibn Sina (980–1037).\nالشرطة الطويلة (—) لإبراز المعلومة بقوة: Her discovery—a new element—changed medicine.\nنستخدم العلامات في أزواج وسط الجملة، ولا نضع فواصل حول المعلومة الضرورية."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Al-Khwarizmi—the Father of Algebra—Lived in Baghdad: Commas, Parentheses and Dashes”. Let’s learn punctuating extra (nonrestrictive) information: commas around appositives and non-defining clauses, parentheses for less important extra information, dashes for emphasis; using punctuation in pairs; no commas around essential (restrictive) information together.","on_screen":"GV-L07-U3-LS4 | Al-Khwarizmi—the Father of Algebra—Lived in Baghdad: Commas, Parentheses and Dashes","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Extra (nonrestrictive) information can be set off in three ways. Commas are the most common: Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad. (A noun phrase like this, which renames a noun, is called an appositive.)","on_screen":"punctuating extra (nonrestrictive) information: commas around appositives and non-defining clauses, parentheses for less important extra information, dashes for emphasis; using punctuation in pairs; no commas around essential (restrictive) information","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Extra (nonrestrictive) information can be set off in three ways. Commas are the most common: Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad. (A noun phrase like this, which renames a noun, is called an appositive.) Parentheses ( ) add information that is less important – dates, explanations or examples: Ibn Sina (980–1037) wrote about medicine. Dashes (—) make the extra information stronger or more dramatic: Her discovery—a new element—changed medicine. In American English, the long dash (—) usually has no spaces around it.","on_screen":"commas: X, extra information, verb… • parentheses: less important extra details, e.g., (980–1037) • dashes: strong emphasis—like this—in the middle or at the end","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Ibn Rushd, a philosopher from Córdoba, wrote about Aristotle. Al-Biruni (973–1048) calculated the size of the Earth. The answer was simple—it was water!","on_screen":"Ibn Rushd, a philosopher from Córdoba, wrote about Aristotle. / Al-Biruni (973–1048) calculated the size of the Earth. / The answer was simple—it was water!","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Al-Khwarizmi a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad.” That’s not right. We say: “Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad.” Commas come in pairs.","on_screen":"✗ Al-Khwarizmi a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad.   ✓ Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"Ibn Sina (980–1037)\" – are the dates the most important information? … Think! … No – they're extra, less important details. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Al-Khwarizmi, a Persian mathematician, worked in Baghdad.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Al-Khwarizmi, a Persian mathematician, worked in Baghdad.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: punctuating extra (nonrestrictive) information: commas around appositives and non-defining clauses, parentheses for less important extra information, dashes for emphasis; using punctuation in pairs; no commas around essential (restrictive) information.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","المعلومة الإضافية نفصلها بالفواصل غالبًا: Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Al-Khwarizmi a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad. — الصحيح: Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Al-Khwarizmi, a Persian mathematician, worked in Baghdad."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"A Short Biography (editing passage)","body":"Al-Jazari an engineer from northern Mesopotamia wrote a famous book about machines in 1206. His most famous design, the elephant clock used water and floats to measure time. The book which described about fifty machines was later copied many times. One of his designs a hand-washing machine still surprises engineers today.","word_count":51,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.","items":[{"q":"","options":["Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, wrote a famous book on algebra.","Al-Khwarizmi a mathematician, from Persia wrote a famous book on algebra.","Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia wrote a famous book on algebra.","Al-Khwarizmi a mathematician from Persia, wrote a famous book on algebra."],"answer":"Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, wrote a famous book on algebra.","explanation":"Commas around an appositive or extra information.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل حول المعلومة الإضافية."},{"q":"","options":["Ibn Sina, (980–1037), wrote about medicine and philosophy.","Ibn Sina (980–1037) wrote about medicine and philosophy.","Ibn Sina (980–1037 wrote about medicine and philosophy.","Ibn Sina 980–1037) wrote about medicine and philosophy."],"answer":"Ibn Sina (980–1037) wrote about medicine and philosophy.","explanation":"Parentheses for less important extra details.","explanation_ar":"الأقواس."},{"q":"","options":["Her—discovery a new element—changed medicine.","Her discovery—a new element, changed medicine.","Her discovery a new element—changed medicine.","Her discovery—a new element—changed medicine."],"answer":"Her discovery—a new element—changed medicine.","explanation":"Dashes for strong emphasis.","explanation_ar":"الشرطات."},{"q":"Which sentence is missing a comma?","options":["Fez, which is in Morocco has a very old university.","Fez, which is in Morocco, has a very old university.","The university that Fatima built is in Fez.","Fatima al-Fihri built a university in Fez."],"answer":"Fez, which is in Morocco has a very old university.","explanation":"Use the marks in pairs.","explanation_ar":"العلامات في أزواج."},{"q":"","options":["The student who won the science fair, is in my class.","The student who won the science fair is in my class.","The student, who won the science fair, is in my class.","The student, who won the science fair is in my class."],"answer":"The student who won the science fair is in my class.","explanation":"Essential information → no commas.","explanation_ar":"المعلومة الضرورية بلا فواصل."},{"q":"Why are parentheses used in \"Al-Idrisi's map (made in 1154) was very accurate\"?","options":["to show strong emotion","to end the sentence","to add extra, less important information","to show a question"],"answer":"to add extra, less important information","explanation":"Parentheses for less important extra details.","explanation_ar":"الأقواس."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS4-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Add the punctuation in brackets.","items":[{"q":"Optics the science of light fascinated Ibn al-Haytham. (commas)","options":[],"answer":"Optics, the science of light, fascinated Ibn al-Haytham.","explanation":"Commas around an appositive or extra information.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل حول المعلومة الإضافية."},{"q":"Al-Biruni 973–1048 studied astronomy. (parentheses)","options":[],"answer":"Al-Biruni (973–1048) studied astronomy.","explanation":"Parentheses for less important extra details.","explanation_ar":"الأقواس."},{"q":"The answer was simple it was water. (dash)","options":[],"answer":"The answer was simple—it was water.","explanation":"Dashes for strong emphasis.","explanation_ar":"الشرطات."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"Makkah, which is a holy city has millions of visitors every year.","options":[],"answer":"Makkah, which is a holy city, has millions of visitors every year.","explanation":"which is a holy city has → which is a holy city, has. Use the marks in pairs.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: which is a holy city has → which is a holy city, has. العلامات في أزواج."},{"q":"The book , that I'm reading, is about Al-Jazari.","options":[],"answer":"The book that I'm reading is about Al-Jazari.","explanation":", that I'm reading, → that I'm reading. Essential information → no commas.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , that I'm reading, → that I'm reading. المعلومة الضرورية بلا فواصل."},{"q":"My cousin—a talented engineer,built a robot for the competition.","options":[],"answer":"My cousin—a talented engineer—built a robot for the competition.","explanation":", → —. Dashes for strong emphasis.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → —. الشرطات."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the biography.","items":[{"q":"Al-Jazari an engineer from northern Mesopotamia wrote a famous book about machines in 1206.","options":[],"answer":"Al-Jazari, an engineer from northern Mesopotamia, wrote a famous book about machines in 1206.","explanation":"(add) → ,. Commas around an appositive or extra information.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: (add) → ,. الفواصل حول المعلومة الإضافية."},{"q":"Al-Jazari an engineer from northern Mesopotamia wrote a famous book about machines in 1206.","options":[],"answer":"Al-Jazari, an engineer from northern Mesopotamia, wrote a famous book about machines in 1206.","explanation":"(add) → ,. Commas around an appositive or extra information.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: (add) → ,. الفواصل حول المعلومة الإضافية."},{"q":"Al-Jazari an engineer from northern Mesopotamia wrote a famous book about machines in 1206.","options":[],"answer":"Al-Jazari, an engineer from northern Mesopotamia, wrote a famous book about machines in 1206.","explanation":"(add) → ,. Commas around an appositive or extra information.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: (add) → ,. الفواصل حول المعلومة الإضافية."},{"q":"The book which described about fifty machines) was later copied many times.","options":[],"answer":"The book (which described about fifty machines) was later copied many times.","explanation":"which → (which. Commas around an appositive or extra information.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: which → (which. الفواصل حول المعلومة الإضافية."},{"q":"The book (which described about fifty machines was later copied many times.","options":[],"answer":"The book (which described about fifty machines) was later copied many times.","explanation":"(add) → ). Commas around an appositive or extra information.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: (add) → ). الفواصل حول المعلومة الإضافية."},{"q":"One of his designs a hand-washing machine still surprises engineers today.","options":[],"answer":"One of his designs—a hand-washing machine—still surprises engineers today.","explanation":"(space) → —. Commas around an appositive or extra information.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: (space) → —. الفواصل حول المعلومة الإضافية."},{"q":"One of his designs a hand-washing machine still surprises engineers today.","options":[],"answer":"One of his designs—a hand-washing machine—still surprises engineers today.","explanation":"(space) → —. Commas around an appositive or extra information.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: (space) → —. الفواصل حول المعلومة الإضافية."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write with commas, parentheses and dashes.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence about a scholar with an appositive between commas.","options":[],"answer":"Ibn Rushd, a judge and philosopher, lived in Córdoba.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses punctuating extra (nonrestrictive) information: commas around appositives and non-defining clauses, parentheses for less important extra information, dashes for emphasis; using punctuation in pairs; no commas around essential (restrictive) information correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with dates in parentheses.","options":[],"answer":"Ibn Battuta (1304–1369) traveled across Africa and Asia.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses punctuating extra (nonrestrictive) information: commas around appositives and non-defining clauses, parentheses for less important extra information, dashes for emphasis; using punctuation in pairs; no commas around essential (restrictive) information correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with a pair of dashes for emphasis.","options":[],"answer":"The prize—a real telescope—went to our team!","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses punctuating extra (nonrestrictive) information: commas around appositives and non-defining clauses, parentheses for less important extra information, dashes for emphasis; using punctuation in pairs; no commas around essential (restrictive) information correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Letter Ladder","mechanic":"word_builder","rules":["The leader chooses a word from the list and draws one blank line per letter.","Teams take turns guessing letters. Correct letters are written on the lines.","Each wrong guess removes one rung of the ladder (start with 6 rungs).","The team that completes the word scores 2 points and uses it in a sentence."],"data":[{"word":"scholar","clue":"a person who studies a subject deeply and knows a lot about it"},{"word":"discovery","clue":"finding something that nobody knew before"},{"word":"invention","clue":"a new machine or idea that someone creates"},{"word":"experiment","clue":"a scientific test to find out if an idea is true"},{"word":"evidence","clue":"facts that show something is true"},{"word":"philosopher","clue":"a person who thinks deeply about life, truth and knowledge"},{"word":"genius","clue":"a person who is extremely intelligent or talented"},{"word":"theory","clue":"an idea that tries to explain why something happens"},{"word":"observe","clue":"to watch something carefully to learn about it"},{"word":"analyze","clue":"to study something in detail to understand it"},{"word":"contribution","clue":"something you give or do that helps a project or a field"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"","options":["Marie Curie, a Polish scientist won two Nobel Prizes.","Marie Curie, a Polish scientist, won two Nobel Prizes.","Marie Curie, a Polish, scientist won two Nobel Prizes.","Marie Curie a Polish scientist, won two Nobel Prizes."],"answer":"Marie Curie, a Polish scientist, won two Nobel Prizes.","explanation":"Commas around an appositive or extra information.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل حول المعلومة الإضافية."},{"q":"","options":["Hegra (also called Mada'in Salih) is in Al-Ula.","Hegra also called Mada'in Salih) is in Al-Ula.","Hegra (also called Mada'in Salih is in Al-Ula.","Hegra, (also called Mada'in Salih), is in Al-Ula."],"answer":"Hegra (also called Mada'in Salih) is in Al-Ula.","explanation":"Parentheses for less important extra details.","explanation_ar":"الأقواس."},{"q":"","options":["Only one thing could save—the experiment more time.","Only one thing could save the experiment, more time—.","Only one thing could save the experiment—more time.","Only one thing—could save the experiment more time."],"answer":"Only one thing could save the experiment—more time.","explanation":"Dashes for strong emphasis.","explanation_ar":"الشرطات."},{"q":"Which sentence uses commas correctly?","options":["The scientists who discovered the vaccine, were praised.","The scientists, who discovered the vaccine were praised.","The scientists, who discovered, the vaccine were praised.","The scientists who discovered the vaccine were praised."],"answer":"The scientists who discovered the vaccine were praised.","explanation":"Essential information → no commas.","explanation_ar":"المعلومة الضرورية بلا فواصل."},{"q":"What is missing? \"Baghdad, the capital of the Abbasid Empire was a center of learning.\"","options":["nothing","a comma after \"Empire\"","a period after \"capital\"","a comma after \"Baghdad\""],"answer":"a comma after \"Empire\"","explanation":"Use the marks in pairs.","explanation_ar":"العلامات في أزواج."},{"q":"Which mark gives the strongest emphasis to extra information?","options":["commas","dashes","periods","parentheses"],"answer":"dashes","explanation":"Dashes for strong emphasis.","explanation_ar":"الشرطات."},{"q":"","options":["Our robot which took three months to build—won first prize.","Our robot—which took three months to build, won first prize.","Our robot—which took three months to build—won first prize.","Our—robot which took three months to build—won first prize."],"answer":"Our robot—which took three months to build—won first prize.","explanation":"Use the marks in pairs.","explanation_ar":"العلامات في أزواج."},{"q":"","options":["Abbas ibn Firnas, (810–887) tried to fly with wings.","Abbas ibn Firnas 810–887) tried to fly with wings.","Abbas ibn Firnas (810–887) tried to fly with wings.","Abbas ibn Firnas (810–887 tried to fly with wings."],"answer":"Abbas ibn Firnas (810–887) tried to fly with wings.","explanation":"Parentheses for less important extra details.","explanation_ar":"الأقواس."},{"q":"","options":["My favorite, subject chemistry is also the hardest.","My favorite subject chemistry, is also the hardest.","My favorite subject, chemistry is also the hardest.","My favorite subject, chemistry, is also the hardest."],"answer":"My favorite subject, chemistry, is also the hardest.","explanation":"Commas around an appositive or extra information.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل حول المعلومة الإضافية."},{"q":"Which sentence should have no extra commas?","options":["The girl who won the spelling bee is my neighbor.","My neighbor, Lina, won the spelling bee.","Lina, who won the spelling bee, is my neighbor.","Lina, my neighbor, won the spelling bee."],"answer":"The girl who won the spelling bee is my neighbor.","explanation":"Essential information → no commas.","explanation_ar":"المعلومة الضرورية بلا فواصل."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Great Minds” that shows punctuating extra (nonrestrictive) information: commas around appositives and non-defining clauses, parentheses for less important extra information, dashes for emphasis; using punctuation in pairs; no commas around essential (restrictive) information in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Great Minds” that shows punctuating extra (nonrestrictive) information: commas around appositives and non-defining clauses, parentheses for less important extra information, dashes for emphasis; using punctuation in pairs; no commas around essential (restrictive) information in action"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Letter Ladder”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Letter Ladder"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Al-Khwarizmi—the Father of Algebra—Lived in Baghdad: Commas, Parentheses and Dashes”. Let’s learn punctuating extra (nonrestrictive) information: commas around appositives and non-defining clauses, parentheses for less important extra information, dashes for emphasis; using punctuation in pairs; no commas around essential (restrictive) information together. Extra (nonrestrictive) information can be set off in three ways. Commas are the most common: Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad. (A noun phrase like this, which renames a noun, is called an appositive.) Extra (nonrestrictive) information can be set off in three ways. Commas are the most common: Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad. (A noun phrase like this, which renames a noun, is called an appositive.) Parentheses ( ) add information that is less important – dates, explanations or examples: Ibn Sina (980–1037) wrote about medicine. Dashes (—) make the extra information stronger or more dramatic: Her discovery—a new element—changed medicine. In American English, the long dash (—) usually has no spaces around it. Here are some examples. Ibn Rushd, a philosopher from Córdoba, wrote about Aristotle. Al-Biruni (973–1048) calculated the size of the Earth. The answer was simple—it was water! Be careful! Some learners say: “Al-Khwarizmi a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad.” That’s not right. We say: “Al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician from Persia, worked in Baghdad.” Commas come in pairs. Quick check: \"Ibn Sina (980–1037)\" – are the dates the most important information? … Think! … No – they're extra, less important details. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Al-Khwarizmi, a Persian mathematician, worked in Baghdad.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS5","title":"A Great Mind I Admire: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to present a short biography of a great thinker.","Students will be able to write a biography paragraph with relative clauses, academic words and correctly punctuated extra information.","I can present a great mind to my class.","I can write a short biography with extra information."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تقديم سيرة قصيرة لمفكر عظيم.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة فقرة سيرة بجمل الوصل والكلمات الأكاديمية والمعلومات الإضافية مرقّمة بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع أن أقدّم أحد العظماء لصفّي.","أستطيع أن أكتب سيرة قصيرة فيها معلومات إضافية."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"scholar","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈskɑlɚ/","definition":"a person who studies a subject deeply and knows a lot about it","arabic":"عالِم / باحث","example":"Ibn Sina was a great scholar of medicine.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"genius","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdʒinjəs/","definition":"a person who is extremely intelligent or talented","arabic":"عبقري","example":"Al-Khwarizmi was a mathematical genius.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"discovery","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈskʌvɚi/","definition":"finding something that nobody knew before","arabic":"اكتشاف","example":"The discovery of penicillin saved millions of lives.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"invention","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪnˈvɛnʃən/","definition":"a new machine or idea that someone creates","arabic":"اختراع","example":"The water clock was an amazing invention.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"experiment","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪkˈspɛrəmənt/","definition":"a scientific test to find out if an idea is true","arabic":"تجربة علمية","example":"We did an experiment with light and mirrors.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"theory","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈθɪri/","definition":"an idea that tries to explain why something happens","arabic":"نظرية","example":"His theory explained how we see.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"evidence","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɛvədəns/","definition":"facts that show something is true","arabic":"دليل / برهان","example":"Scientists need evidence to support their ideas.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"observe","pos":"verb","ipa":"/əbˈzɝv/","definition":"to watch something carefully to learn about it","arabic":"يلاحظ / يرصد","example":"Al-Biruni observed the sky for many years.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"analyze","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈænəˌlaɪz/","definition":"to study something in detail to understand it","arabic":"يحلّل","example":"The students analyzed the results of the test.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"contribution","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌkɑntrəˈbjuʃən/","definition":"something you give or do that helps a project or a field","arabic":"إسهام","example":"Muslim scholars made a huge contribution to science.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"astronomy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈstrɑnəmi/","definition":"the scientific study of stars, planets and space","arabic":"علم الفلك","example":"Astronomy helped travelers find their way at night.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"philosopher","pos":"noun","ipa":"/fəˈlɑsəfɚ/","definition":"a person who thinks deeply about life, truth and knowledge","arabic":"فيلسوف","example":"Ibn Rushd was a famous philosopher from Córdoba.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"manuscript","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmænjəˌskrɪpt/","definition":"a very old book or document written by hand","arabic":"مخطوطة","example":"The library keeps rare Arabic manuscripts.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"translate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/trænzˈleɪt/","definition":"to change words from one language into another","arabic":"يترجم","example":"His books were translated into Latin.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 3: non-defining clauses (, who…,), defining clauses (the students who…), academic word families and roots, and commas, parentheses and dashes for extra information.\nA biography gives essential facts (who, when, where, what they did) and extra information that makes it interesting. Use relative clauses and appositives to combine facts into rich, smooth sentences.\n✔ non-defining: commas, no that • defining: no commas\n✔ discovery, invention, contribution, scholar, scientific\n✔ (dates) • —emphasis— • appositive commas","examples":["Ibn Sina, who was born near Bukhara, studied medicine as a teenager.","Al-Khwarizmi (about 780–850) worked at the House of Wisdom.","The book that made him famous was about algebra.","Al-Jazari's machines—over fifty of them—were described in one book.","Al-Biruni, a scholar from Central Asia, wrote about India's culture and science.","Her contribution to education is still remembered.","He made an important discovery about light.","Al-Zahrawi's book, which had thirty volumes, was used in Europe."],"common_errors":["✗ Fatima al-Fihri who was born in Kairouan moved to Fez. → ✓ Fatima al-Fihri, who was born in Kairouan, moved to Fez. (Non-defining → commas.)","✗ The students, who studied there, became teachers. (only some) → ✓ The students who studied there became teachers. (Defining → no commas.)","✗ Her contribute to education was huge. → ✓ Her contribution to education was huge. (After her → noun.)"],"arabic":"نراجع جمل الوصل المحدِّدة وغير المحدِّدة والكلمات الأكاديمية والجذور وعلامات الترقيم للمعلومات الإضافية.\nالسيرة تذكر الحقائق الأساسية (مَن، متى، أين، ماذا فعل) ومعلومات إضافية تجعلها ممتعة.\nتذكّر: فواصل حول غير المحدِّدة، ولا فواصل حول المحدِّدة، وأقواس للتواريخ."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “A Great Mind I Admire: Language in Use”. Let’s learn non-defining and defining relative clauses; academic word families and roots; commas, parentheses and dashes (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L07-U3-LS5 | A Great Mind I Admire: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Great Minds gallery walk: each group prepares a panel about one scholar or scientist (name, dates in parentheses, two non-defining clauses, one defining clause, two academic words). Groups walk around, read the panels and ask one question at each.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 3: non-defining clauses (, who…,), defining clauses (the students who…), academic word families and roots, and commas, parentheses and dashes for extra information. A biography gives essential facts (who, when, where, what they did) and extra information that makes it interesting. Use relative clauses and appositives to combine facts into rich, smooth sentences.","on_screen":"✔ non-defining: commas, no that • defining: no commas • ✔ discovery, invention, contribution, scholar, scientific • ✔ (dates) • —emphasis— • appositive commas","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Ibn Sina, who was born near Bukhara, studied medicine as a teenager. Al-Khwarizmi (about 780–850) worked at the House of Wisdom. The book that made him famous was about algebra.","on_screen":"Ibn Sina, who was born near Bukhara, studied medicine as a teenager. / Al-Khwarizmi (about 780–850) worked at the House of Wisdom. / The book that made him famous was about algebra.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Fatima al-Fihri who was born in Kairouan moved to Fez.” That’s not right. We say: “Fatima al-Fihri, who was born in Kairouan, moved to Fez.” Non-defining → commas.","on_screen":"✗ Fatima al-Fihri who was born in Kairouan moved to Fez.   ✓ Fatima al-Fihri, who was born in Kairouan, moved to Fez.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “The students, who studied there, became teachers. (only some)”? … Think! … It should be “The students who studied there became teachers.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can present a great mind to my class.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can present a great mind to my class.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: non-defining and defining relative clauses; academic word families and roots; commas, parentheses and dashes (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع جمل الوصل المحدِّدة وغير المحدِّدة والكلمات الأكاديمية والجذور وعلامات الترقيم للمعلومات الإضافية.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Fatima al-Fihri who was born in Kairouan moved to Fez. — الصحيح: Fatima al-Fihri, who was born in Kairouan, moved to Fez.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقدّم أحد العظماء لصفّي."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Fatima al-Fihri – A Woman Who Built a University","body":"Fatima al-Fihri, who was born in Kairouan around 800, moved to Fez, Morocco, with her family.\nHer father, who was a successful merchant, left her a large fortune.\nIn 859, she used that money to build al-Qarawiyyin, which began as a mosque and soon became a center of learning.\nScholars came from far away to teach astronomy, mathematics and medicine (as well as religion and languages).\nThe students who studied there later shared their knowledge across Africa and Europe.\nToday, al-Qarawiyyin—which is still open—is often described as the oldest existing university in the world.\nFatima's contribution, which many people don't know about, shows how one person's generosity can change a whole city.","word_count":112,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the biography. True or False?","items":[{"q":"Fatima al-Fihri was born in Fez.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Fatima al-Fihri – A Woman Who Built a University”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Her father was a successful merchant.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Fatima al-Fihri – A Woman Who Built a University”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Al-Qarawiyyin began as a mosque.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Fatima al-Fihri – A Woman Who Built a University”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Only religion was taught at al-Qarawiyyin.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Fatima al-Fihri – A Woman Who Built a University”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Al-Qarawiyyin is still open today.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Fatima al-Fihri – A Woman Who Built a University”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Everyone knows about Fatima's contribution.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Fatima al-Fihri – A Woman Who Built a University”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Fatima al-Fihri, ___ was born in Kairouan, moved to Fez.","options":["whose","where","which","who"],"answer":"who","explanation":"Relative clause (defining or non-defining).","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل."},{"q":"Al-Qarawiyyin, ___ began as a mosque, became a center of learning.","options":["where","who","which","whose"],"answer":"which","explanation":"Relative clause (defining or non-defining).","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل."},{"q":"The students ___ studied there shared their knowledge.","options":["which","who","whose",", who"],"answer":"who","explanation":"Relative clause (defining or non-defining).","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل."},{"q":"Fatima's ___ to education is still remembered.","options":["contributor","contribution","contributive","contribute"],"answer":"contribution","explanation":"Academic word or word-family form.","explanation_ar":"كلمة أكاديمية."},{"q":"","options":["Al-Qarawiyyin, which is still open—is very old.","Al-Qarawiyyin which is still open—is very old.","Al-Qarawiyyin—which is still open—is very old.","Al-Qarawiyyin—which is still open, is very old."],"answer":"Al-Qarawiyyin—which is still open—is very old.","explanation":"Commas, parentheses or dashes for extra information.","explanation_ar":"علامات الترقيم."},{"q":"Why are parentheses used in \"(as well as religion and languages)\"?","options":["to add less important extra information","to ask a question","to show surprise","to show the main idea"],"answer":"to add less important extra information","explanation":"Commas, parentheses or dashes for extra information.","explanation_ar":"علامات الترقيم."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Prepare your gallery panel.","items":[{"q":"Name and dates (in parentheses): ___","options":[],"answer":"Al-Jazari (1136–1206)","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining and defining relative clauses; academic word families and roots; commas, parentheses and dashes (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A non-defining clause about where he or she lived or worked: ___","options":[],"answer":"He worked in Diyarbakir, where he served the local rulers.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining and defining relative clauses; academic word families and roots; commas, parentheses and dashes (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A non-defining clause about his or her work: ___","options":[],"answer":"His book, which described fifty machines, was finished in 1206.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining and defining relative clauses; academic word families and roots; commas, parentheses and dashes (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A defining clause: ___","options":[],"answer":"The machine that made him famous was the elephant clock.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining and defining relative clauses; academic word families and roots; commas, parentheses and dashes (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Two academic words you will use: ___","options":[],"answer":"invention, contribution","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining and defining relative clauses; academic word families and roots; commas, parentheses and dashes (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your biography.","items":[{"q":"Plan: name, dates, place, main achievement, two extra facts, why he or she matters.","options":[],"answer":"Al-Biruni (973–1048); Khwarazm, later Ghazni; measured the size of the Earth; wrote about India; spoke many languages; showed the value of careful observation","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining and defining relative clauses; academic word families and roots; commas, parentheses and dashes (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your biography in 7–8 sentences.","options":[],"answer":"Al-Biruni (973–1048) was a scholar who studied almost everything. He was born in Khwarazm, which is in Central Asia. Later he lived in Ghazni, where he wrote many books. He observed the sky carefully and calculated the size of the Earth. His result—amazingly accurate—was close to modern measurements. He also wrote a famous book about India, whose people and science he studied. His contribution to astronomy and geography was enormous. He teaches us that careful observation is the key to discovery.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining and defining relative clauses; academic word families and roots; commas, parentheses and dashes (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: commas around non-defining clauses? no commas around defining clauses? parentheses and dashes in pairs? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I added a second comma after \"Central Asia\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining and defining relative clauses; academic word families and roots; commas, parentheses and dashes (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Treasure Hunt Review","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["Teams move to a station every 90 seconds.","At each station, write one correct sentence with the cue word and non-defining and defining relative clauses; academic word families and roots; commas, parentheses and dashes (unit review).","Teams collect a \"treasure letter\" for each correct sentence.","Unscramble the letters at the end to reveal the secret word."],"data":[{"cue":"scholar","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"genius","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"discovery","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"invention","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"experiment","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"theory","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"evidence","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"observe","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"analyze","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"contribution","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"astronomy","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"philosopher","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"manuscript","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"translate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Ibn Rushd, ___ was also a judge, wrote about philosophy.","options":["that","which","who","whose"],"answer":"who","explanation":"Relative clause (defining or non-defining).","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل."},{"q":"Córdoba, ___ Ibn Rushd was born, was a great city of learning.","options":["which","whose","when","where"],"answer":"where","explanation":"Relative clause (defining or non-defining).","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل."},{"q":"","options":["The scholar who he translated the book knew five languages.","The scholar which translated the book knew five languages.","The scholar, who translated the book knew five languages.","The scholar who translated the book knew five languages."],"answer":"The scholar who translated the book knew five languages.","explanation":"Relative clause (defining or non-defining).","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل."},{"q":"Al-Khwarizmi was a mathematical ___.","options":["genius","genial","geniuses","genie"],"answer":"genius","explanation":"Academic word or word-family form.","explanation_ar":"كلمة أكاديمية."},{"q":"Doctors used Ibn Sina's book as ___ for their treatments.","options":["evidential","evident","evidence","evidently"],"answer":"evidence","explanation":"Academic word or word-family form.","explanation_ar":"كلمة أكاديمية."},{"q":"","options":["Al-Biruni (973–1048 calculated the size of the Earth.","Al-Biruni (973–1048) calculated the size of the Earth.","Al-Biruni, (973–1048) calculated the size of the Earth.","Al-Biruni 973–1048) calculated the size of the Earth."],"answer":"Al-Biruni (973–1048) calculated the size of the Earth.","explanation":"Commas, parentheses or dashes for extra information.","explanation_ar":"علامات الترقيم."},{"q":"","options":["Al-Jazari, a brilliant engineer, designed the elephant clock.","Al-Jazari a brilliant engineer, designed the elephant clock.","Al-Jazari a brilliant, engineer designed the elephant clock.","Al-Jazari, a brilliant engineer designed the elephant clock."],"answer":"Al-Jazari, a brilliant engineer, designed the elephant clock.","explanation":"Commas, parentheses or dashes for extra information.","explanation_ar":"علامات الترقيم."},{"q":"The Arabic numbers we use today—including zero—came to Europe through translations. Why are dashes used?","options":["to show a list","to emphasize extra information","to join two separate sentences","to end a question"],"answer":"to emphasize extra information","explanation":"Commas, parentheses or dashes for extra information.","explanation_ar":"علامات الترقيم."},{"q":"Al-Idrisi's world map, ___ he made for a king in Sicily, was very detailed.","options":["where","which","who","whose"],"answer":"which","explanation":"Relative clause (defining or non-defining).","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل."},{"q":"Pick the best sentence for a biography:","options":["Ibn al-Haytham (965–1040), whose experiments changed science, lived in Cairo for many years.","Ibn al-Haytham, (965–1040), that experiments changed science, lived in Cairo.","Ibn al-Haytham (965–1040) whose experiments changed science lived in Cairo for many years.","Ibn al-Haytham, who his experiments changed science, lived in Cairo."],"answer":"Ibn al-Haytham (965–1040), whose experiments changed science, lived in Cairo for many years.","explanation":"Relative clause (defining or non-defining).","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A \"Hall of Great Minds\" display in the school library with portrait-free panels (books, instruments and maps instead of faces) for Fatima al-Fihri, Al-Khwarizmi, Ibn Sina and Al-Jazari; Sara presenting with a pointer. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A \"Hall of Great Minds\" display in the school library with portrait-free panels (books, instruments and maps instead of faces) for Fatima al-Fihri, Al-Khwarizmi, Ibn Sina and Al-Jazari; Sara presenting with a pointer"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Great Minds”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Great Minds”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L07-U3-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Treasure Hunt Review”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Treasure Hunt Review"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS5-T1","script":"Fatima al-Fihri, who was born in Kairouan around 800, moved to Fez, Morocco, with her family. Her father, who was a successful merchant, left her a large fortune. In 859, she used that money to build al-Qarawiyyin, which began as a mosque and soon became a center of learning. Scholars came from far away to teach astronomy, mathematics and medicine (as well as religion and languages). The students who studied there later shared their knowledge across Africa and Europe. Today, al-Qarawiyyin—which is still open—is often described as the oldest existing university in the world. Fatima's contribution, which many people don't know about, shows how one person's generosity can change a whole city.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L07-U3-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “A Great Mind I Admire: Language in Use”. Let’s learn non-defining and defining relative clauses; academic word families and roots; commas, parentheses and dashes (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Great Minds gallery walk: each group prepares a panel about one scholar or scientist (name, dates in parentheses, two non-defining clauses, one defining clause, two academic words). Groups walk around, read the panels and ask one question at each. Today you use all of Unit 3: non-defining clauses (, who…,), defining clauses (the students who…), academic word families and roots, and commas, parentheses and dashes for extra information. A biography gives essential facts (who, when, where, what they did) and extra information that makes it interesting. Use relative clauses and appositives to combine facts into rich, smooth sentences. Here are some examples. Ibn Sina, who was born near Bukhara, studied medicine as a teenager. Al-Khwarizmi (about 780–850) worked at the House of Wisdom. The book that made him famous was about algebra. Be careful! Some learners say: “Fatima al-Fihri who was born in Kairouan moved to Fez.” That’s not right. We say: “Fatima al-Fihri, who was born in Kairouan, moved to Fez.” Non-defining → commas. Quick check: what is wrong with “The students, who studied there, became teachers. (only some)”? … Think! … It should be “The students who studied there became teachers.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can present a great mind to my class.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"Al-Zahrawi, ___ lived in Córdoba, was a brilliant surgeon.","options":["where","whose","who","which"],"answer":"who","explanation":"Person → who.","explanation_ar":"للعاقل who."},{"q":"The astrolabe, ___ helped sailors find their way, was improved by Muslim scientists.","options":["who","whose","when","which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"Thing or idea → which (not that).","explanation_ar":"لغير العاقل which."},{"q":"Abbas ibn Firnas, ___ dream was to fly, built a pair of wings.","options":["who","who's","whose","which"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"Possession (his/her/its) → whose.","explanation_ar":"للملكية whose."},{"q":"Damascus, ___ many scholars studied, is one of the oldest cities in the world.","options":["when","which","where","whose"],"answer":"where","explanation":"Place → where.","explanation_ar":"للمكان where."},{"q":"","options":["The night sky, that Al-Biruni studied for years, was full of questions.","The night sky, which Al-Biruni studied for years, was full of questions.","The night sky, which Al-Biruni studied it for years, was full of questions.","The night sky which Al-Biruni studied for years was full of questions."],"answer":"The night sky, which Al-Biruni studied for years, was full of questions.","explanation":"Commas around the extra information; no extra pronoun.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل ولا ضمير زائد."},{"q":"","options":["The girl who designed the app is only fourteen.","The girl which designed the app is only fourteen.","The girl, who designed the app, is only fourteen.","The girl who she designed the app is only fourteen."],"answer":"The girl who designed the app is only fourteen.","explanation":"Defining (which one?) → no commas.","explanation_ar":"المحدِّدة بلا فواصل."},{"q":"\"My aunt who works at the hospital is a surgeon.\" What does this suggest?","options":["I have more than one aunt.","My aunt doesn't work.","My aunt is a patient.","I have only one aunt."],"answer":"I have more than one aunt.","explanation":"Commas change the meaning.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل تغيّر المعنى."},{"q":"Which sentence would still be correct without its relative pronoun?","options":["Mars, which we studied, is red.","The theory that we studied was difficult.","The scientist who studied Mars is famous.","Al-Biruni, who studied the sky, was a genius."],"answer":"The theory that we studied was difficult.","explanation":"Leave out the object pronoun only in defining clauses.","explanation_ar":"الحذف في المحدِّدة فقط."},{"q":"The experiment failed three times, ___ made us more determined.","options":["who","what","that","which"],"answer":"which","explanation":", which comments on the whole clause.","explanation_ar":"which للتعليق على الجملة كلها."},{"q":"","options":["The Moon, which has no light of its own, reflects sunlight.","The Moon, that has no light of its own, reflects sunlight.","The Moon which has no light of its own reflects sunlight.","The Moon, which it has no light of its own, reflects sunlight."],"answer":"The Moon, which has no light of its own, reflects sunlight.","explanation":"Non-defining (extra) → commas.","explanation_ar":"غير المحدِّدة بفواصل."},{"q":"Scientists test their ideas with careful ___.","options":["philosophers","manuscripts","geniuses","experiments"],"answer":"experiments","explanation":"Commas change the meaning.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل تغيّر المعنى."},{"q":"\"Micro\" in \"microscope\" means ___.","options":["far","life","star","small"],"answer":"small","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root to find the meaning.","explanation_ar":"الجذر يدل على المعنى."},{"q":"The ___ of the telephone changed communication forever.","options":["inventive","invention","invent","inventor"],"answer":"invention","explanation":"Choose the right word-family form.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الصحيحة."},{"q":"She studies living things. She is a ___.","options":["biologically","biological","biology","biologist"],"answer":"biologist","explanation":"The word for the person (-er, -or, -ist, -ian).","explanation_ar":"كلمة الشخص."},{"q":"The doctors did a careful ___ of the test results.","options":["analysis","analytical","analyze","analyzed"],"answer":"analysis","explanation":"Choose the right word-family form.","explanation_ar":"الصيغة الصحيحة."},{"q":"Which word is the study of the Earth's surface, countries and maps?","options":["geography","astronomy","biography","biology"],"answer":"geography","explanation":"Use the Greek/Latin root to find the meaning.","explanation_ar":"الجذر يدل على المعنى."},{"q":"","options":["Ibn Sina, a doctor and philosopher wrote The Canon of Medicine.","Ibn Sina a doctor, and philosopher wrote The Canon of Medicine.","Ibn Sina, a doctor and philosopher, wrote The Canon of Medicine.","Ibn Sina a doctor and philosopher, wrote The Canon of Medicine."],"answer":"Ibn Sina, a doctor and philosopher, wrote The Canon of Medicine.","explanation":"Commas around an appositive or extra information.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل حول المعلومة الإضافية."},{"q":"","options":["Al-Khwarizmi about 780–850) worked at the House of Wisdom.","Al-Khwarizmi, (about 780–850), worked at the House of Wisdom.","Al-Khwarizmi (about 780–850 worked at the House of Wisdom.","Al-Khwarizmi (about 780–850) worked at the House of Wisdom."],"answer":"Al-Khwarizmi (about 780–850) worked at the House of Wisdom.","explanation":"Parentheses for less important extra details.","explanation_ar":"الأقواس."},{"q":"","options":["The secret ingredient patience—made our project a success.","The secret ingredient—patience—made our project a success.","The secret ingredient—patience, made our project a success.","The secret—ingredient patience—made our project a success."],"answer":"The secret ingredient—patience—made our project a success.","explanation":"Dashes for strong emphasis.","explanation_ar":"الشرطات."},{"q":"Which sentence has a punctuation mistake?","options":["Riyadh is growing fast.","Riyadh, the capital of Saudi Arabia is growing fast.","The capital of Saudi Arabia is Riyadh.","Riyadh, the capital of Saudi Arabia, is growing fast."],"answer":"Riyadh, the capital of Saudi Arabia is growing fast.","explanation":"Use the marks in pairs.","explanation_ar":"العلامات في أزواج."},{"q":"","options":["The scientist, who discovered insulin, won a Nobel Prize.","The scientist who discovered insulin, won a Nobel Prize.","The scientist who discovered insulin won a Nobel Prize.","The scientist, who discovered insulin won a Nobel Prize."],"answer":"The scientist who discovered insulin won a Nobel Prize.","explanation":"Essential information → no commas.","explanation_ar":"المعلومة الضرورية بلا فواصل."},{"q":"Which sentence would make the best opening for a biography?","options":["Al-Idrisi, (1100–1165), that traveled widely, made a world map.","Al-Idrisi (1100–1165) who traveled widely made a world map, that was used for centuries.","Al-Idrisi (1100–1165), who traveled widely, made a world map that was used for centuries.","Al-Idrisi who he traveled widely made a world map."],"answer":"Al-Idrisi (1100–1165), who traveled widely, made a world map that was used for centuries.","explanation":"Commas around the extra information; no extra pronoun.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل ولا ضمير زائد."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"ibn battuta who traveled for thirty years wrote a famous book.\"","options":["Ibn Battuta who traveled for thirty years, wrote a famous book.","Ibn Battuta, who traveled for thirty years, wrote a famous book.","ibn Battuta, who traveled for thirty years, wrote a famous book.","Ibn Battuta, that traveled for thirty years, wrote a famous book."],"answer":"Ibn Battuta, who traveled for thirty years, wrote a famous book.","explanation":"Commas around the extra information; no extra pronoun.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل ولا ضمير زائد."},{"q":"Al-Biruni observed the sky and analyzed the data carefully, ___ is why his results were accurate.","options":["which","that","what","who"],"answer":"which","explanation":", which comments on the whole clause.","explanation_ar":"which للتعليق على الجملة كلها."}],"theme_ar":"عقول عظيمة","essential_question_ar":"ماذا نتعلّم من عظماء المفكرين في الماضي؟","can_do":"I can add extra information with non-defining relative clauses, tell defining from non-defining clauses, use academic word families and Greek/Latin roots, and punctuate extra information with commas, parentheses and dashes.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أضيف معلومات إضافية بجمل الوصل غير المحدِّدة، وأميّز بينها وبين جمل الوصل المحدِّدة، وأستخدم عائلات الكلمات الأكاديمية والجذور اليونانية واللاتينية، وأرقّم المعلومات الإضافية بالفواصل والأقواس والشرطات."});
